Keep loop-carried matrix accumulators in place across the back edge.

A loop that rescales a WMMA accumulator lane by lane and multiplies into it,
as online-softmax attention does, copied every accumulator register on the
back edge each iteration. Three allocation decisions caused this:

- low-decompose-cfg-tuples split the loop-carried tuple into independent
  lanes, which allocation could not place back into the aligned tuple the
  matrix instruction requires. A back edge that forwards a tuple its producer
  defines whole now keeps the argument whole; joins outside loops and lanes
  assembled by low.concat still decompose.
- A loop result is read after the loop, so each lane's linear end point
  spanned the whole body even after its last use there. Unit liveness now
  records each unit's last use per semantic segment for multi-unit values
  with several segments. Conflict and coalescing checks release a unit after
  that use unless the value is live out of the segment. Values whose storage
  outlives their segments keep the linear lifetimes.
- Concat reservations took the first free aligned span. A reservation whose
  result reaches a branch destination through copies and tied results now
  tries that destination's location first.

With all three, the rescale multiplies write the lanes they read, the WMMA
accumulates in place, and the back edge carries no copies. In the krea2 fp16
attention kernel on gfx1151, 56 of 77 per-iteration back-edge moves go away.
The kernel runs 1.007-1.073x faster from 1040 to 9000 tokens with
byte-identical output. Unrelated kernels, such as the int8 GEMMs, compile to
identical code objects. The added assembly test fails without the allocation
changes, and the added pass test fails without the decomposition change.
Existing codegen, AMDGPU target, transform and tooling tests are unchanged.

